---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const steps=[['Install Learning Site Editor','Download the current Windows installer and complete the installation.'],['Connect existing repository','Select the local legacy repository or clone it from its remote. Do not copy content into a new project.'],['Legacy project detection','The editor inspects the repository and identifies the legacy Learning Site structure before any upgrade is applied.'],['Safe upgrade','Confirm the upgrade. Your user content is preserved, and the editor creates a Git recovery point before migration so the earlier state remains recoverable.'],['Preview','Open the local preview and check pages, links, media, theme appearance, and language direction.'],['Publish','When the preview is right, commit and push. Your normal GitHub Pages or Cloudflare Pages deployment can publish the upgraded static output.']];
---
<Base title="Migrate a legacy Learning Site"><section class="page-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Legacy migration</p><h1>Move forward without losing your work.</h1><p class="lede">Migration is designed around one non-negotiable: your content stays yours. A Git recovery point is created before the project is updated.</p></section><section class="migration-steps">{steps.map(([title,copy], index)=><article><b>{index+1}</b><div><h2>{title}</h2><p>{copy}</p></div></article>)}</section><section class="callout"><h2>Keep the recovery point.</h2><p>Do not delete the migration commit or its history. It is your straightforward route back if you need to compare or restore the legacy state.</p><a class="button light" href="/download/">Download the editor</a></section></Base>
